description:
Every cached method on *fantrax.Client re-types the same eight lines: short-circuit on empty cacheDir, construct a FileCache with a hand-picked TTL, build the key, call Get with a one-line closure delegating to the uncached fetchXxx sibling. 20 such branches exist across 23 cache.New call sites.

Representative pair -- these two are byte-for-byte identical apart from the key constant and the delegate name:

  client.go:334          func (c *Client) GetHitterRoster() ([]Player, error) {
                           if c.cacheDir == "" { return c.fetchHitterRosterForPeriod(0) }
                           fc := cache.New[[]Player](c.cacheDir, c.todayTTL)
                           key := cache.Key(keyHitterRoster, c.teamID)
                           return fc.Get(key, func() ([]Player, error) {
                             return c.fetchHitterRosterForPeriod(0)
                           })
                         }
  pitcher_roster.go:27   func (c *Client) GetPitcherRoster() ([]Player, error) { ...identical shape... }

Sites: client.go:33,334,348,427,462,507,620,659 · pitcher_roster.go:27,41,79,118 · gs_limits.go:36 · recent_stats.go:74 · pitcher_recent_stats.go:92 · daily_fpts.go (getPeriodSnapshotCached) · gs_check.go (getPlayerGSSnapshotForPeriodCached) and the GetHitterRosterForPeriod / GetPitcherRosterForPeriod per-period variants.

Cache KEY grammar is already centralised as constants in internal/fantrax/cachekeys.go:8-27, explicitly so the <source>-<entity>[-scope...] naming is enforceable in one place. The TTL CHOICE and the branch itself were never given the same treatment -- so the three-tier TTL scheme documented in CLAUDE.md (pastPeriodTTL 30d / stableTTL 7d / todayTTL 15m, mapping to three mutability classes) exists only in prose. Which tier a method gets is decided by whichever constant the author typed, with nothing to check it.

gs_limits.go:36 shows the cost of the copy-paste: because GetGSLimits returns (min, max *int, err), the unwrap has to be written twice, once per branch.

Surfaced by the 2026-07-22 architecture review (candidate 6 of 7).

design:
One generic helper on *Client owns the cache-plumbing policy; each method names only its key, its tier and its fetch.

Sketch (shape to be settled in implementation -- Go method-level generics are not available, so this is likely a package-level generic function taking the client):

    func cached[T any](c *Client, keyPrefix string, ttl time.Duration, scope []string, fetch func() (T, error)) (T, error)

    func (c *Client) GetHitterRoster() ([]Player, error) {
        return cached(c, keyHitterRoster, c.todayTTL, []string{c.teamID},
            func() ([]Player, error) { return c.fetchHitterRosterForPeriod(0) })
    }

Points to settle during implementation:
  - Make the TTL tier a named type (e.g. a cacheTier enum resolving to a duration) rather than a raw time.Duration, so the three-tier scheme is expressed in the type and a fourth ad-hoc TTL has to be a deliberate choice. Note the provider-specific TTLs that legitimately sit outside the scheme (projections.ProjectionCacheTTL, statcast.CacheTTL, hkb 8h, prospect rankings) -- those live in other packages and are out of scope here.
  - Per-period methods call c.ttlForPeriod(period) rather than a constant; the helper must accommodate a computed TTL, not just a tier.
  - Multi-return methods (GetGSLimits) need the helper to work over a single value type with the unwrap outside it -- which is already how the cached branch works today, it just is not shared.
  - The empty-cacheDir short-circuit is load-bearing for --no-cache AND is how several tests stay hermetic; it must behave identically.

This is mechanical and low risk: no behaviour change, no interface change to any caller of *Client, ~160 lines of implementation deleted.
